 "readme": "# FIPC Pilot — Fidelity-Induced Privacy Collapse (Gate 1)\n\nThe 48-hour experiment that decides whether the FIPC project is a go.\n\n**Question:** when a commercial LLM generates \"privacy-preserving\" synthetic data\nin a *generate → validate → repair* loop, does record-level leakage of the source\n**rise as the fidelity/utility validators get stricter** — and does the LLM leak\n**more than a non-copying statistical baseline** at the same validator levels?\n\nIf yes → the effect exists and is non-tautological → build the full study.\nIf no → retune once, else pivot. You'll know in a weekend for ~$30.\n\n---\n\n## Run it\n\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n\n# Offline first — no API key, no cost. Validates the whole pipeline on a mock LLM:\npython run_pilot.py --dry-run --trials 5\npython plot_pilot.py            # -> fipc_pilot_curve.png\n\n# Live (one OpenRouter key covers GPT / Claude / Gemini):\nexport OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-...\npython run_pilot.py --model openai/gpt-5        --out gpt5.json\npython run_pilot.py --model anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5 --out claude.json\npython run_pilot.py --model google/gemini-2.5-pro       --out gemini.json\npython plot_pilot.py gpt5.json\n```\n\nCost control for the live run: defaults are 6 levels × 5 trials × up to 4 repair\nrounds ≈ a few hundred calls per model. Start with `--trials 2` to smoke-test cost.\n\n## What the run prints\n\n1. **Anti-tautology oracle** — whether a non-copying conditional resampler\n   (`StatBaseline`) can pass each validator level. **Every level must say PASS.**\n   If a level FAILS, that level structurally forces copying and is scientifically\n   worthless — loosen it before trusting any result. (This is the objection a\n   reviewer will raise first; the pilot checks it for you.)\n2. **Leakage vs validator level** — the effect. `*_excess` = metric on the source\n   (shown to model) minus the same metric on a **holdout** never shown to anyone.\n   Positive, rising excess = genuine record-level leakage, not population similarity.\n3. **Gate-1 verdict** — EFFECT PRESENT if L5 near-copy excess ≥ 0.10 or ≥2× over L0.\n\n## Files\n\n| File | Role |\n|------|------|\n| `source_data.py` | Synthetic source + holdout + planted canaries (all fake data). |\n| `validators.py` | Validator ladder L0–L5 (schema→marginals→corr→tail→utility). |\n| `generators.py` | `LLMGenerator` (repair loop) + `StatBaseline` (oracle/baseline) + mock. |\n| `leakage.py` | Exact/near copy, canary reproduction, **excess-over-holdout**. |\n| `run_pilot.py` | Orchestrates the sweep, prints oracle + curve + verdict. |\n| `plot_pilot.py` | Draws `fipc_pilot_curve.png` (embryo of the paper's Fig. 1). |\n\n## The three design choices that make this defensible (don't remove them)\n\n1. **Holdout control.** Every leakage metric is reported as source-minus-holdout.\n   This is what separates real leakage from \"the LLM makes realistic-looking rows.\"\n2. **Anti-tautology oracle.** A non-copying resampler must be able to pass every\n   level, proving the leakage the LLM shows is *excess*, not forced by the task.\n3. **Planted canaries.** Unambiguous, unique records → exact-reproduction is\n   impossible to hand-wave away.\n\n## After Gate 1 (not in this pilot)\n\nFull study adds: the paired include/exclude MIA (causal identification),\nmatched-utility frontier vs DP synthesizers, 3 models × more trials, rare-vs-common\nsplit, and variance over ≥5 seeds. See `FIPC_battle_plan.md`.\n\n## Ethics\n\nAll \"private\" data here is synthetic, generated from a known process. No real\nperson's data is used; leakage is measured against records we planted. This studies\na workflow *vulnerability* defensively — it produces no harmful content.\n",
